    "scienceEn": "Saffron is the world's costliest spice: it takes roughly 150,000 to 200,000 delicate violet crocus flowers harvested entirely by hand at dawn to produce just 1 kilogram of dried saffron threads. Its sensory properties stem from three key chemical compounds: 1) Crocin: a carotenoid pigment that dissolves readily in warm water/broth turning it brilliant luminous gold; 2) Safranal: volatile essential oil creating the warm hay-like aroma; 3) Picrocrocin: glucoside delivering its characteristic bittersweet taste.",
